  "text": "The sea had just closed on an army. She picked up a tambourine. Exodus fifteen. Israel has walked through the sea on dry ground and watched it come down behind them. Now, order matters here, so let us be accurate. Moses and the children of Israel sing first. That is verse one. Then verse twenty. \"And Miriam the prophetess, the sister of Aaron, took a timbrel in her hand. And all the women went out after her with timbrels and with dances.\" And Miriam answered them. Answered. She takes the song and leads the women in it, with percussion and dancing, standing on the edge of a crossing that should not have been possible. \"Sing ye to the Lord, for he hath triumphed gloriously. The horse and his rider hath he thrown into the sea.\" Two things Scripture calls her, right there, in one verse. Prophetess. And sister of Aaron. Now here is the thing readers connect. Exodus two never names the sister who watched a baby float away in a basket and talked a princess into hiring his own mother as the nurse. Numbers twenty-six names Miriam among those siblings. That is the link most people make. It is a reasonable reading, not a stated one. What is not in doubt is this. When the water closed, she was the one who reached for an instrument.",
